      "question": {
        ":question/id": "65409563-6aa3-541a-86d5-30b4fe746231",
        ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=-2{cos}^{6}\u2061(x),$ we have\n\n${f}^{\u2032}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express\n\n$$\nf(x)=-2(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(x))^{6}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=-2{u}^{6},\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=-12{u}^{5},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(x).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-12{u}^{5}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(x)) \\\\  & =12{cos}^{5}\u2061(x)\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
